course: biology
lesson: 219
title: Butterflies, moths and ladybirds
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A fat green caterpillar munches leaves for weeks, then hangs up and hardens into a chrysalis, and inside that little case its body breaks down and is rebuilt into a butterfly, like a house taken apart and built again as something new.
explain: A butterfly lands on a leaf and tastes it with its feet, to check whether it is the right plant for its eggs, and it basks in the sun because a cold butterfly cannot fly.
explain: Its wings are covered with thousands of tiny scales, like the tiles on a roof, and those scales are the powder that comes off on your fingers if you touch one.
explain: Moths are the butterflies' night-time cousins; only the caterpillars of a few kinds ever eat clothes, and the moths circling a lamp are not in love with it but confused by it.
explain: A ladybird's bright red and black coat is a warning sign that says «I taste horrible», and every gardener loves it because it eats thousands of the aphids that suck the plants.
fact: A caterpillar turns into a butterfly inside a chrysalis, where its body breaks down and is rebuilt.
fact: Butterflies taste with their feet; a female uses them to check that a leaf is the right one for her eggs.
fact: Butterflies rest in the sun to warm up, because they cannot fly when they are too cold.
fact: A butterfly's wings are covered in tiny scales; the powder on your fingers is scales, and losing many of them can harm the butterfly.
fact: Moths get confused by lamps; a 2024 study reported that they turn their backs towards the brightest light, as they would towards the sky, and so circle it.
fact: Only the caterpillars of a few kinds of moth eat wool clothes; most moths never touch clothes at all.
fact: A ladybird's bright red and black colours warn birds that it tastes bad, and when attacked it oozes a smelly yellow fluid from its legs.
fact: Ladybirds eat aphids, and one ladybird can eat thousands of aphids in its life.
fact: Ladybirds hibernate in winter in groups, often in hollow stems, under bark or in window frames, and huddling helps them survive the cold.
fact: A peacock butterfly flashes the big eye spots on its wings to scare away birds, because the spots look like the eyes of a large animal.
fact: Buddleia, called the butterfly bush, is full of nectar and attracts many butterflies to a garden.
fact: Butterflies, moths and ladybirds are insects, with six legs and two pairs of wings; a ladybird's front pair are hard red covers over its thin flying wings.
fact: A moth's caterpillar often makes a silk cocoon before it changes, but a butterfly's caterpillar makes a bare chrysalis.
exercise: Where does a caterpillar change into a butterfly? | Inside a chrysalis | Inside a bird's egg | Under the pond water | Inside a flower bud
exercise: What happens to the caterpillar's body inside the chrysalis? | It breaks down and is rebuilt as a butterfly | It just grows wings and keeps everything else | It sleeps and stays exactly the same | It shrinks into a tiny egg
exercise: How does a butterfly taste a leaf? | With its feet | With its wings | With its antennae only | It cannot taste at all
exercise: Why does a butterfly taste leaves before laying eggs? | To check it is the right plant for its caterpillars | To find the sweetest leaf in the garden to eat itself | To see if the leaf is wet enough to drink | To make sure no bird is sitting on the leaf
exercise: Why does a butterfly sit with its wings open in the sun? | To warm up, because it cannot fly when cold | To dry its wings after drinking nectar | To show off its bright colours to people | To stop the sun reaching the flowers below
exercise: What is the powder that comes off a butterfly's wing? | Tiny scales that cover the wing | Pollen picked up from the flowers | Dust blown up from the ground | Dried nectar
exercise: Why should you not touch a butterfly's wings? | It can rub off scales and harm the butterfly | The powder on them is poisonous to people | The wings will sting your fingers like a nettle | It will make the butterfly change colour for good
exercise: Why do moths fly round and round a lamp? | They get confused and keep their backs to the light | They are trying to eat the light for energy | They want to warm up their eggs | They love the colour of the bulb and want to sit on it
exercise: Which moths eat woollen clothes? | Only the caterpillars of a few kinds | All grown-up moths, of every kind | Every moth that comes indoors | Moths that fly round lamps
exercise: What do a ladybird's bright red and black colours tell a bird? | I taste horrible, leave me alone | I am a sweet ripe berry, come and eat me | I am a baby bird, please look after me | I am poisonous to touch, but tasty
exercise: What does a ladybird do when a bird attacks it? | It oozes a smelly yellow fluid from its legs | It stings the bird hard with its tail | It changes colour to green to hide among leaves | It bites the bird with big jaws
exercise: Why are gardeners happy to see ladybirds? | They eat thousands of aphids that harm plants | They eat the weeds' leaves | They make the flowers grow taller and brighter | They scare the slugs and snails away from the lettuces
exercise: Why do ladybirds huddle together in winter? | Huddling in groups helps them survive the cold | They are waiting to fly south together for the winter | They are laying one giant egg | They are fighting over food
exercise: Why does a peacock butterfly flash its eye spots? | To scare birds, because the spots look like big eyes | To see better with its extra eyes | To warm up its wings in the sun | To call other butterflies over to a party on the flowers
exercise: Why do people plant buddleia? | Its nectar-rich flowers attract butterflies | Its leaves keep moths out of clothes cupboards | It kills aphids on other plants | Butterflies build nests in it
exercise: How many wings does a butterfly have? | Four, in two pairs | Two, one on each side | Six, three on each side | Eight, like a spider's legs
test: «there's 3 butterflies on the buddleia how many legs then» | 18 | 12 | 24 | 6
test: In a famous picture book a hungry caterpillar builds a cocoon before it becomes a butterfly. What is more accurate? | A butterfly caterpillar makes a chrysalis; cocoons are mostly made by moths | A butterfly caterpillar lays an egg that hatches into a butterfly | A butterfly caterpillar grows wings while still eating leaves | A butterfly caterpillar turns into a moth first
test: «mum says moths ate her jumper, so we gotta kill every moth in the garden» What is right? | No, only the caterpillars of a few kinds eat wool | Yes, every moth eats clothes | Yes, garden moths fly in at night to eat jumpers | No, it was butterflies that ate it, not moths
test: What would happen if a butterfly woke up on a cold, cloudy morning? | It would have to warm up before it could fly | It would fly faster and faster to keep warm | It would turn back into a caterpillar | It would go back to sleep until next summer comes
test: «caught a butterfly, its wing rubbed off on my fingers like glitter» What has happened? | Scales came off, which can harm the butterfly | Pollen from the flowers fell off it | The butterfly sprayed glitter to escape | Nothing, butterflies want to lose that powder
test: A bird bites a ladybird once and spits it out. What will the bird probably do next time it sees red and black spots? | Leave the ladybird alone | Eat two ladybirds at once | Peck it harder to get the juice | Bring it home to its hungry chicks
test: Count them: a ladybird eats 50 aphids a day. How many is that in 10 days? | 500 | 60 | 5,000 | 150
test: «ladybirds all piled up in the window frame?? is that a nest or what» What is really going on? | They are hibernating together through the winter | They are building a nest out of mud | They are trapped and dying | They are laying their eggs in the glass
test: In a cartoon a moth flies at a lamp because it wants to eat the light. What is true? | Real moths get confused and keep their backs to the light | Real moths do eat light to make energy | Real moths fly at lamps to lay eggs on them | Real moths think lamps are big flowers full of sweet nectar
test: What would most likely happen to a robin that spotted a peacock butterfly suddenly flashing its eye spots? | It might get a fright and fly away | It would think it was another robin | It would fall fast asleep on the spot | It would bring all its friends to eat it
test: «tasting with feet? that's gross, makes no sense» Why is it useful for a butterfly? | It can check a leaf for its eggs just by landing on it | It can taste the ground to find water | It can eat while it is flying, without ever landing | It can tell if it is going to rain
exam: Identify the process by which the larva of the butterfly is transformed into the adult insect. | The body is broken down and reconstructed within a chrysalis | The larva acquires wings while continuing to feed | The larva deposits an egg from which the adult subsequently emerges | The larva is transformed within the stomach of a bird
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the damage to woollen garments attributed to moths? | It is caused solely by the larvae of a small number of species | It is caused by the adults of all species of moth | It is caused by moths attracted to illuminated rooms in the evening | It is caused by butterflies mistaken for moths
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the coloration of the ladybird. | The bright coloration serves to warn predators of an unpleasant taste | The bright coloration serves to attract aphids towards the insect | The bright coloration serves to absorb heat during the winter | The bright coloration serves to conceal the insect among berries
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a butterfly is observed to rest with its wings extended in direct sunlight. | The insect must attain a sufficient body temperature before flight | The insect is engaged in the drying of freshly consumed nectar | The insect is displaying its colours to human observers in the garden | The insect is depriving competing plants of sunlight
